  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Smoke continues to impact the region, though it is slowly\n  thinning out. \n\n- Warming, drying trend continues through the week with Friday or\n  Saturday as the hottest day of the week. \n\n- Breezy conditions at the end of the week pose a fire weather\n  risk starting Friday. \n\nA ridge continues to build over the western U.S. the next few\ndays. This is causing a warming and drying trend as well as stable\nconditions. The stable conditions will keep smoke from area\nwildland fires in the area, but fire activity has slightly reduced\nwith the lack of wind under the ridge so it won't be as thick as a\nfew days ago. Models have recently trended slower with a\nflattening out of the ridge this weekend, and now either Friday or\nSaturday looks to be the hottest day of the week. \n\nWhen the ridge finally does flatten out, it will cause breezy\nconditions. While this will help ventilate some of the smoke out\nof the area better, it will also make conditions more difficult\nfor wildland firefighters and may result in more smoke production.\nOnce the ridge flattens out, temperatures will cool by about 3-5\ndegrees, so it won't be much of a respite. \n\nOverall, there isn't any rain in the forecast for the foreseeable\nfuture. That being said, over the next couple of weeks\nprecipitable water trends up. That means if a disturbance comes\nalong, it will have moisture to work with to cause some rain. Long\nrange models are hinting at some potential beyond day 7 of the\nforecast, but even that looks meager.",
